      "rank": 1,
      "ballot_id": "B007",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Harald Rose", "Knut Urban", "Maximilian Haider"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Spherical-aberration correction transformed electron microscopy, enabling atomic-scale imaging and analysis that substantially expanded what could be measured in materials and nanoscience. It is a strong case for recognizing an instrument advance that opened new experimental access.",
        "attribution": "Rose's optical theory and sustained advocacy, with Urban and Haider's experimental realization and validation, form a credible core attribution. The history spans additional contributors and instrument development, so the specific boundaries of credit deserve care.",
        "maturity": "The correction has been implemented in working instruments for decades, with extensive use and reproducible improvements in resolution and measurement capability.",
        "uncertainties": ["The relative weight of the theoretical proposal, early practical correction, and later commercial instruments in the original invention is historically complex."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Michel Della Negra", "Peter Jenni", "Tejinder Virdee"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The experimental discovery of the Higgs boson completed a central prediction of the Standard Model and confirmed the mechanism associated with particle masses. It is a landmark experimental achievement.",
        "attribution": "These three leaders are plausible representatives of the ATLAS and CMS discovery effort, but the observation depended on large collaborations whose contributions cannot be reduced neatly to a few individuals.",
        "maturity": "The discovery was announced in 2012 and has since been supported by extensive measurements of the particle's properties.",
        "uncertainties": ["The Nobel's individual laureate limit makes fair recognition of the two collaborations a major attribution challenge."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B016",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alfred Y. Cho", "Federico Capasso", "Jérôme Faist"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum cascade laser was a distinctive invention based on engineered semiconductor transitions, enabling compact sources across important infrared and terahertz bands with broad scientific and practical uses.",
        "attribution": "Capasso and Faist are central to the device concept and demonstration; Cho's role in the enabling semiconductor growth technology is substantial. The precise division between enabling technology and invention merits historical review.",
        "maturity": "The device was demonstrated in 1994 and has undergone decades of development and reliable use.",
        "uncertainties": ["The boundaries of credit among the core inventors and contributors to semiconductor epitaxy and device implementation need careful reconstruction."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Heino Falcke", "J. Anton Zensus", "Sheperd S. Doeleman"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Event Horizon Telescope's image of the M87 black hole provided direct, compelling evidence of the shadow-scale structure expected around a black hole and demonstrated extraordinary global interferometry.",
        "attribution": "Falcke's theoretical proposal and the leadership of Zensus and Doeleman represent important strands of the achievement, while the image depended on a large international team and many technical contributions.",
        "maturity": "The 2019 image was followed by additional observations, including Sagittarius A*, strengthening the method and astrophysical interpretation.",
        "uncertainties": ["The relation between the observed emission structure and the black hole shadow requires modeling, and the individual attribution within the collaboration is difficult."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B018",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Eli Yablonovitch", "Sajeev John", "Susumu Noda"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Photonic crystals and photonic band gaps established a powerful way to control light through periodic structure, with deep consequences for optics and a wide range of device research.",
        "attribution": "Yablonovitch and John independently articulated influential photonic band-gap concepts; Noda's work helped develop and demonstrate practical photonic-crystal structures. The distinct strands should be reflected in the credit.",
        "maturity": "The concepts have been studied and applied for decades, with extensive experimental demonstrations and continuing technological development.",
        "uncertainties": ["The proposed discovery groups together independent theoretical proposals and later demonstrations; the precise scope of the original discovery needs definition."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B014",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Kenneth J. Schafer", "Maciej Lewenstein", "Paul B. Corkum"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The recollision model explained how strong laser fields drive electrons back to their parent ions, providing a physical foundation for high-harmonic generation and attosecond measurement.",
        "attribution": "Corkum's recollision picture and the related theoretical work of Schafer and Lewenstein are central, but the field grew through closely connected theory and experiments by many groups.",
        "maturity": "The model has been tested and refined across decades of experiments and underlies a mature research field.",
        "uncertainties": ["The contribution overlaps conceptually with the attosecond science recognized by the 2023 Physics Prize, and the boundaries of the proposed discovery require careful comparison."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B061",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Charles L. Bennett", "David N. Spergel", "Lyman A. Page"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "WMAP's precise full-sky measurements of cosmic microwave background anisotropies tightly constrained the parameters and history of the universe, making a major observational contribution to precision cosmology.",
        "attribution": "Bennett and Page represent key instrument and mission leadership; Spergel made a prominent contribution to interpretation. The mission's result was produced by a broad team, and individual credit should reflect the actual division of work.",
        "maturity": "Its measurements have been independently compared with later CMB observations and remain a foundational cosmological data set.",
        "uncertainties": ["Some of WMAP's conclusions depend on a cosmological model and parameter assumptions; attribution across the large mission team is also nontrivial."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B027",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Jonathan C. Knight", "Philip St. J. Russell", "Timothy A. Birks"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Photonic crystal fibres made new control of light propagation possible and expanded fibre optics into applications such as unusual dispersion engineering and guidance of light in a hollow core.",
        "attribution": "Russell's conceptual and experimental work is central, with Knight and Birks among the key early experimental contributors. The attribution should distinguish the original fibre demonstration from subsequent variants.",
        "maturity": "The first demonstrations date to the 1990s, followed by sustained development and use in research and technology.",
        "uncertainties": ["The candidate spans a family of fibre designs, and the contribution of each proposed laureate may depend on which specific invention is recognized."]
      }
    }
  ]
